update articles set
  title_en = $t$Travel Health Guide: Vaccines, Insurance and Avoiding Illness$t$,
  excerpt_en = $e$Stay healthy abroad: vaccines, travel insurance, medicines, a travel pharmacy checklist, avoiding stomach bugs and heatstroke, and what to do in emergencies.$e$,
  body_en = $b$<p>Nothing ruins a trip faster than falling ill far from home. The good news is that most travel health problems, such as stomach upsets, heat exhaustion, jet lag and minor injuries, are predictable and largely preventable. This guide explains how to prepare before you leave, what to pack, how to avoid the most common travel illnesses and what to do if something goes wrong abroad, whether you are heading for a European city break, a beach in South-East Asia or a safari in Africa.</p>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> this article is general information, not medical advice. Always consult a doctor or a travel health clinic about your own situation, especially if you are pregnant, travelling with a baby, or living with a chronic condition.</p>

<h2>Before You Travel: A Health Checklist</h2>
<h3>See a Doctor 4 to 8 Weeks Before Departure</h3>
<p>Some vaccines need several weeks to become effective or require more than one dose. A travel health consultation lets a professional review your destination, the length and style of your trip and your medical history. Ask about:</p>
<ul>
<li>Routine vaccinations (for example tetanus, measles and seasonal flu) and whether yours are up to date.</li>
<li>Destination-specific vaccines, such as hepatitis A, typhoid, rabies or Japanese encephalitis, depending on where and how you travel.</li>
<li>Whether you need malaria prevention for parts of Sub-Saharan Africa, South and South-East Asia, Latin America or other regions.</li>
<li>A yellow fever vaccination certificate, which some countries require at entry, particularly if you arrive from a country with yellow fever risk.</li>
</ul>
<p>Reliable official sources include the WHO, the US CDC's Travelers' Health pages, NHS Fit for Travel and TravelHealthPro in the UK, and Smartraveller in Australia. They list recommendations country by country.</p>
<h3>Pilgrims: Umrah and Hajj</h3>
<p>Saudi authorities set specific health requirements for pilgrims, including meningococcal vaccination. Requirements can be updated each season, so check the official Saudi guidance and your national health authority well before departure.</p>
<h3>Travel Insurance</h3>
<p>Medical treatment abroad can be very expensive. In the United States in particular, a short hospital stay can cost many thousands of dollars. Good travel insurance should cover emergency treatment, hospitalisation, repatriation and, ideally, trip cancellation. Keep in mind:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Schengen visa applicants</strong> must generally show travel medical insurance valid across the Schengen Area with a minimum level of cover, commonly 30,000 EUR.</li>
<li><strong>Reciprocal healthcare cards</strong>, such as the European Health Insurance Card (EHIC) for EU residents and the UK's Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC), give access to necessary state healthcare in certain countries. They do not replace travel insurance and do not cover repatriation.</li>
<li><strong>Pre-existing conditions</strong> must usually be declared, or they may not be covered.</li>
</ul>
<p>Insurance prices vary with age, destination, trip length and cover. Policies that include the USA and Canada usually cost more. Always compare what is actually covered, not just the price.</p>

<h2>Travelling with Medicines</h2>
<ul>
<li>Bring enough of your regular medicines for the whole trip plus a few extra days in case of delays.</li>
<li>Keep them in their original packaging, in your hand luggage, with a copy of your prescription. A translation or an international non-proprietary name (generic name) helps abroad.</li>
<li>Some medicines that are freely available in one country are controlled in another. The UAE, Saudi Arabia, Japan, Singapore and some other countries restrict certain painkillers, sleeping tablets, ADHD medicines, decongestants and medicines containing codeine. Check the embassy or health ministry website of your destination before travelling.</li>
<li>For injectable medicines such as insulin, carry a doctor's letter and inform security staff at the airport.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Your Travel Pharmacy</h2>
<p>Pack a small kit adapted to your trip. A typical kit might include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Oral rehydration salts for diarrhoea and dehydration.</li>
<li>Paracetamol or another pain reliever and fever reducer that you have used before.</li>
<li>Plasters, antiseptic wipes, a small bandage and blister pads.</li>
<li>Antihistamines for allergies and insect bites.</li>
<li>Motion sickness tablets if you are prone to car, boat or plane sickness.</li>
<li>High-SPF sunscreen, lip balm and after-sun.</li>
<li>Insect repellent containing an ingredient such as DEET, picaridin (icaridin) or similar.</li>
<li>Hand sanitiser and a digital thermometer.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask your pharmacist or doctor which products are suitable for you and your children.</p>

<h2>Common Travel Illnesses and How to Avoid Them</h2>
<h3>Traveller's Diarrhoea</h3>
<p>This is one of the most common travel health problems, usually caused by contaminated food or water. To reduce the risk:</p>
<ul>
<li>Wash your hands before eating and use sanitiser when soap is unavailable.</li>
<li>Choose busy food stalls and restaurants where food is cooked fresh and served hot.</li>
<li>Where tap water is not considered safe, drink bottled or properly treated water and be careful with ice.</li>
<li>Follow the old rule for raw fruit and vegetables: "cook it, peel it or leave it".</li>
</ul>
<p>Most cases settle in a few days with rest and fluids. Drink plenty and use oral rehydration salts. See a doctor quickly if you have blood in your stool, a high fever, signs of severe dehydration or symptoms lasting more than a few days, and for young children, older people and pregnant women.</p>
<h3>Heat Exhaustion and Heatstroke</h3>
<p>Summer heat in southern Europe, the Gulf, North Africa, parts of the USA and Australia can be intense. Drink water regularly, wear a hat and light clothing, avoid strenuous activity in the hottest hours (roughly late morning to late afternoon) and rest in the shade. Confusion, a very high body temperature, hot dry skin or fainting can signal heatstroke, a medical emergency. Call emergency services and cool the person immediately.</p>
<h3>Jet Lag</h3>
<p>Crossing several time zones, for example between Europe and Asia, Australia or the Americas, disrupts your sleep. Shift your sleep schedule gradually a few days before departure, stay hydrated on the plane, limit caffeine, get daylight on arrival and try to adopt local meal and sleep times quickly.</p>
<h3>Motion Sickness</h3>
<p>Ferries, small boats and winding mountain roads can all trigger nausea. Sit where movement is least felt (the middle of a ship or the front of a car), look at the horizon, avoid reading and consider medication taken in advance.</p>
<h3>Mosquito and Insect Bites</h3>
<p>Mosquitoes can transmit diseases such as dengue or malaria in some regions, including parts of Asia, Africa and Latin America. Use repellent, wear long sleeves in the evening and sleep under a net or in screened, air-conditioned rooms where advised.</p>
<h3>Altitude Sickness</h3>
<p>Above roughly 2,500 metres, for example in the Andes, the Himalayas, on Kilimanjaro or in high Alpine areas, some people develop headache, nausea and breathlessness. Ascend gradually, stay hydrated and descend if symptoms worsen.</p>

<h2>Seasonal Health Risks</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Season / situation</th><th>Main risks</th><th>Key precautions</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Summer in southern Europe, the Gulf, North Africa</td><td>Heat, dehydration, sunburn</td><td>Water, shade, sunscreen, rest at midday</td></tr>
<tr><td>Winter in the northern hemisphere</td><td>Seasonal flu and respiratory infections</td><td>Vaccination where recommended, hand hygiene</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rainy seasons in the tropics</td><td>Mosquito-borne diseases</td><td>Repellent, covering clothes, nets</td></tr>
<tr><td>Long-haul flights</td><td>Dehydration, deep vein thrombosis risk</td><td>Walk, stretch, drink water, ask your doctor if at higher risk</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What to Do in a Medical Emergency Abroad</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Know the emergency numbers:</strong> 112 works across the EU and in many other countries. Common examples include 999 in the UK, 911 in the USA and Canada, 000 in Australia, and 998 for an ambulance or 999 for police in the UAE. Verify the numbers for your destination before you go.</li>
<li><strong>Call your insurer early:</strong> most insurers have a 24/7 assistance line and may need to approve treatment or direct you to partner hospitals.</li>
<li><strong>Keep documents to hand:</strong> passport, insurance details, prescriptions and a list of allergies and conditions, stored both on paper and on your phone.</li>
<li><strong>Use pharmacies for minor problems:</strong> in much of Europe and many other countries, pharmacists are a valuable first point of advice, and duty pharmacies often operate at night.</li>
<li><strong>Contact your embassy or consulate</strong> if you are seriously ill, hospitalised or need help contacting family.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>Do I need vaccines to travel abroad?</h3>
<p>It depends on where you are going and what you will do there. Many destinations require none for entry, but you should be up to date with routine vaccinations, and some trips call for extra vaccines or malaria tablets. Check official sources such as the CDC, WHO or NHS Fit for Travel and ask a doctor.</p>
<h3>Is tap water safe to drink abroad?</h3>
<p>In most of Western Europe, North America, Australia, Japan and Singapore, tap water is treated and considered safe. Elsewhere, or if in doubt, follow local guidance or drink bottled water.</p>
<h3>Can I take my prescription medicine on the plane?</h3>
<p>Generally yes, in your hand luggage, in original packaging, with your prescription. Liquids above the usual cabin limit may need to be declared at security. Check rules for controlled substances at your destination.</p>
<h3>Does the EHIC or GHIC replace travel insurance?</h3>
<p>No. These cards give access to necessary public healthcare on the same terms as residents in participating countries, but they do not cover private care, repatriation or trip cancellation.</p>
<h3>What should I do if I get food poisoning while travelling?</h3>
<p>Rest, drink plenty of fluids with oral rehydration salts and eat light food when you can. Seek medical help if symptoms are severe, include blood or high fever, or last more than a few days.</p>

<h2>Travel Healthy with Fastamor</h2>
<p>Good health planning is part of good trip planning. The <strong>Fastamor AI assistant</strong> can help you build a checklist for your destination, find flights from your city, plan realistic daily schedules that avoid the midday heat and organise the documents you need. Tell Fastamor where you are going and start preparing for a safe, healthy journey.</p>$b$,
  updated_at = now()
where slug = 'travel-health-guide' and status = 'published';
